al control\n- forces us to preserve previous state correctly\nThe acceptance pattern should be:\n```text\ncapture current music volume\n→ VoiceBridge active / physical trigger\n→ duck to configured level\n→ interaction ends\n→ restore exact previous volume\n```\nNot “restore to some default.”\n## What I would test first\nBefore buying anything exotic:\n1. **MIDI fader / knob**\n2. Python or lightweight adapter\n3. normalized event\n4. Windows audio/session control\n5. duck / restore\n6. then map a second control to something non-audio, like **focus seat 6**\nIf both work through the same event contract, the architecture is proven.\nThen the hardware audition becomes fun instead of foundational:\n- cheap MIDI controller\n- Genki Wave\n- MiMU glove\n- camera + MediaPipe\n- other gesture devices\n## The key next artifact\nWhen this lane reopens, I would not start with hardware shopping.\nI’d start with a tiny **Control Event Contract**, something like:\n```json\n{\n  \"source\": \"midi|gesture|voice|touch\",\n  \"control\": \"fader|rotate|tap|cue|swipe\",\n  \"value\": 0.42,\n  \"direction\": \"up|down|left|right|clockwise|counterclockwise\",\n  \"velocity\": 0.31,\n  \"duration_ms\": 480,\n  \"confidence\": 0.96,\n  \"context\": \"music|seat|handoff|global\"\n}\n```\nThen the interpreter maps that to:\n```text\nAUDIO_DUCK\nAUDIO_RESTORE\nFOCUS_SEAT\nROUTE_HANDOFF\nNEXT_HANDOFF\nHOLD_HANDOFF\nCUE_SEAT\n```\nThat is the actual heart of the system.\nAnd the most exciting part is that the **same contract can accept a physical fader, a ring, a glove, a camera gesture, or VoiceBridge** without changing the downstream orchestration model.\nThat’s the right place to freeze this for tonight.\n\n13\n00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:02,600\nuser: Good morning buddy\n\n14\n00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:02,800\nassistant: Morning. Sounds like yesterday's mixer and gesture-control deep dive is still rattling around for you.\n\n15\n00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:03,000\nuser: Nah, we're doing good, man. We'