l**.\n\nThe important distinction is:\n\n> **18 processes ≠ 18 browser windows**\n\nThe fastest way to see exactly what they are is Brave’s own task manager:\n\n```text\nShift + Esc\n```\n\nThat will show you which Brave process belongs to:\n- the tab\n- GPU\n- extensions\n- service workers\n- background tasks\n\nIf you want, paste that list and I can tell you which Brave processes are normal and which ones are unnecessary."]}, "create_time": 1790401300.521549, "end_turn": true, "id": "efd4220e-8e85-4bd4-a71b-db440498e994", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "wmrwbh", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}}, "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab43fca21d0819183966405a724bf5d", "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "9c85b3c5-6540-48c0-94f3-5a1fad1c9197", "request_id": "f4a50db9-19ce-497c-8c44-1200abecc401", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "turn_exchange_id": "89371639-a7a3-4c2e-84d5-71303bfc203b", "working_turn_id": "89371639-a7a3-4c2e-84d5-71303bfc203b"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790401312.559094, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["I, I hate... Yeah, but I hate that. That's, that's flawful. But that's... I hate that. This is a flaw in Windows 11. I hate that. We may dial back to Windows 10."]}, "create_time": 1790401318.385134, "end_turn": null, "id": "82722b57-7ac7-4f27-a197-522d176e1fd8", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "dictation": true, "dictation_auto_submitted": true, "dictation_original_text": "I, I hate... Yeah, but I hate that. That's, that's flawful. But that's... I hate that. This is a flaw in Windows 11. I hate that. We may dial back to Windows 10.", "message_source": null, "request_id": "45c2024a-8e5d-40b2-85d5-9dcf25c41eda", "triggered_by_system_hint_suggestion": false, "turn_exchange_id": "543b0585-899c-4e79-a939-f0134b680fbe", "working_turn_id": "543b0585-899c-4e79-a939-f0134b680fbe"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790401330.660155, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["I get why you hate it, but that specific “18 Brave processes for one window” behavior is **not really a Windows 11 flaw**. It’s mostly **Chromium/Brave’s architecture**. You’d see the same basic multi-process behavior on Windows 10, macOS, and Linux too.\n\nSo rolling back to Windows 10 would **not** turn Brave back into one-process-per-window. You’d still get separate renderer/GPU/extension/network processes.\n\nWhat *would* help more than an OS rollback is trimming Brave itself:\n\n- disable unused extensions\n- turn off **Continue running background apps when Brave is closed**\n- turn off or reduce **Memory Saver exceptions**\n- kill unnecessary service workers/background tabs\n- use Brave’s `Shift + Esc` task manager to see which processes are actually chewing RAM/CPU\n\nAlso, Windows 10 is already past mainstream support and at/after end-of-support for normal consumer use by this point, so I would not downgrade just for this browser behavior.\n\nGiven what we just fixed, I’d keep the focus on the stuff that actually mattered: **neutral PowerShell, bounded OpenCode workstream roots, and no provider junk globally injected into the shell.** That was the real machine-killer. Brave’s process count is annoying, but it’s a different issue."]}, "create_time": 1790401322.037896, "end_turn": true, "id": "1b1c5f84-9819-41e3-8b23-a00319e096f1", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "ci