,000\nuser [text]: Here, you're gonna love this. This is the actual detail I was working on separately to fold into this.\nAbsolutely. Here’s the deep handoff specifically for the Don Orchestrated DevPad control-surface lane — mixer board, audio ducking, DJ-style faders/channels, and wearable/gesture control.\nGCE DON ORCHESTRATED DEVPAD — CONTROL SURFACE / MIXER / GESTURE HANDOFF\nDATE: 2026-09-23\nAUTHORITY: Don\nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance\nWORKSTREAM: GCE Stack → Don Orchestrated DevPad → Physical / Gesture Control Surface\nSTATUS: CONCEPT MATURE ENOUGH FOR DEDICATED DESIGN LANE\nCONFIDENCE: 97%\n⸻\n1. PURPOSE\nThis handoff preserves the conceptual state around turning the Don Orchestrated DevPad into more than a screen-based command center.\nThe idea is to give Don a physical orchestration layer capable of controlling:\n* music volume;\n* VoiceBridge interaction;\n* audio ducking;\n* seat focus;\n* routing;\n* cueing;\n* mode/state changes;\n* potentially individual AI/seat channels;\n* eventually hand/finger gesture control.\nThe concept came from Don’s DJ/control-board intuition:\nTreat orchestration more like working a mixer than clicking through software.\nThe goal is not to build a DJ rig for its own sake.\nThe goal is to exploit mature DJ/MIDI/gesture-control hardware as an input surface for GCE orchestration.\n⸻\n2. CURRENT GCE CONTEXT\nThe current Main DevPad is the working command-center surface.\nVerified/current layout:\nCoda      | Chiave      | Vittorio\nNico      | VoiceBridge | Luce\nTalon     | Keel        | Falcon\nCore identity invariant:\nTILE != SEAT != MODEL != RUNTIME != PROVIDER\nand especially:\nMODEL != SEAT\nThe DevPad is the work surface.\nThe separate higher-level orchestration concept has been called:\n* myDon Control\n* Don Controller\n* now more naturally Don Orchestrated DevPad\nThe orchestration layer sits above individual panes/seats.\nIt should control the existing system rather than replace seat authority or create another competing routing architecture.\n⸻\n3. RELATED DEVPAD STRUCTURE\nA two-pad operating concept has emerged.\nMain DevPad\nStable project/canonical work.\nPurpose:\n* durable project sessions;\n* stable seats;\n* current authoritative work;\n* command-center operation.\nRookie DevPad\nBackup / ad hoc / experimental surface.\nPurpose:\n* scratch work;\n* one-offs;\n* temporary model auditions;\n* experimental sessions;\n* things that should not contaminate the Main DevPad.\nThe physical orchestration concept should eventually be able to address both, but that convergence has not been designed yet.\nDo not assume one physical control necessarily maps permanently to one pad.\n⸻\n4. THE MIXER IDEA\nDon proposed using something conceptually similar to a DJ mixer/control board.\nThe important insight was not “DJ equipment.”\nIt was the interaction model.\nA mixer provides:\n* multiple channels;\n* dedicated physical controls;\n* relative adjustment rather than binary commands;\n* fast changes without leaving the primary task;\n* tactile state;\n* muscle memory;\n* faders;\n* knobs;\n* cue buttons;\n* mutes;\n* channel selection;\n* crossfading / blending.\nThose primitives translate surprisingly well into orchestration.\nConceptually:\nPHYSICAL CONTROL\n      ↓\nnormalized control event\n      ↓\nDon orchestration layer\n      ↓\nbounded target/action\n      ↓\nDevPad / VoiceBridge / Windows / audio / seat\n⸻\n5. AUDIO DUCKING — ORIGINAL HIGH-VALUE USE CASE\nThe first concrete use case was simple and immediately useful:\nDon is working with music playing. He invokes VoiceBridge. Music automatically ducks. VoiceBridge interaction occurs. Music returns afterward.\nConcept:\nNormal state:\nMusic = normal volume\nDon invokes VoiceBridge:\nMusic ↓\nVoiceBridge active\nVoice interaction ends:\nMusic ↑ smoothly to prior level\nThis was enthusiastically accepted because it removes a constant ergonomic interruption.\nThe broader Voice Command Plane discussed previously 